<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Food I Eat During a Celebration – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 5px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Festival Season.</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> Food I Eat During a Celebration</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Create a title or caption for a paragraph or image. Reading: Show comprehension of simple texts. Phonemic Awareness: Recognize all the letters of the alphabet in isolation and the correspondence between letter and sound at the beginning of words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can create a title or caption for a drawing or image related to festival foods or activities (e.g., writing "Delicious Food at the Festival" for a picture of festival foods). Can show comprehension of short, simple sentences about festivals and celebrations (e.g., "Look at the fireworks."). Can recognize the initial letter and its corresponding sound in festival-related words (e.g., identifying the letter "P" and its /p/ sound in "party").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; text-align: left;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of festival foods (cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob) from Lesson 1. Students chorally repeat the names. The teacher writes the words on the board next to the pictures. Then, the teacher shows a picture of "fireworks" and "meal." Students identify them, and the teacher writes the words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the word "fireworks" on the board and says, "Fire-works. F-f-f... F is for fireworks." The teacher models the /f/ sound. Then, the teacher points to "meal" and says, "M-m-m... M is for meal." The teacher models the /m/ sound. The teacher reads a simple sentence: "I like cotton candy. It is sweet." and points to each word as it is read. The teacher asks students to repeat the sentence chorally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to "cotton candy" flashcard and the word) "Is this corn on the cob or cotton candy?" (Expected: Cotton candy!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to the word "fireworks") "What sound is this letter? /f/ or /m/?" (Expected: /f/!)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful poster or projected image titled "Festival Food Fun" featuring 3-4 simple sentences about different festival foods with corresponding pictures. The text uses target vocabulary (e.g., festival, shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob, fun, yummy, bright) and grammar (Subject + verb + adjective).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Example Text:</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"Look! It is a festival.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">I like shaved ice. It is cold and yummy!</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">This is cotton candy. It is colorful and sweet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">I also like corn on the cob. It is big and delicious.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The festival is fun!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of short, simple sentences about festivals and celebrations. Phonemic Awareness: Can recognize the initial letter and its corresponding sound in festival-related words (e.g., identifying the letter "P" and its /p/ sound in "party").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to each word and picture. Students follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the text about?" (Expected: Festival food!)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (TPR):</b> The teacher reads simple statements and students give a 'thumbs up' for True or 'thumbs down' for False (e.g., "The shaved ice is hot." "The cotton candy is colorful.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> The teacher asks, "What is cold and yummy?" Students point to "shaved ice" in the text or on the poster.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading simple texts to show comprehension. Identifying initial sounds of words like 'party' /p/, 'fireworks' /f/, 'meal' /m/. Using Subject + verb + adjective and Present simple (e.g., "I like the meal.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Matching Words to Pictures):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with pictures of festival foods (shaved ice, cotton candy, corn on the cob) and a list of their written names. Students draw lines to match each food picture to its correct word.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Reading & Circling):</b> Students receive another worksheet with simple sentences from the Presentation text, but with a missing word (cloze activity) and two choices to circle. For example: "I like _____. (shaved ice / balloons)." or "The festival is _____. (sad / fun)." Students read the sentence and circle the correct word. The teacher monitors and helps with reading.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Phonemic Reading):</b> The teacher writes three words on the board: "party", "fireworks", "meal". Students read each word aloud. The teacher asks, "What is the first sound in 'party'?" (Expected: /p/!). "What letter makes that sound?" (Expected: P!). Students then underline the initial letter of each word. The teacher repeats for "fireworks" /f/ and "meal" /m/. Students then read simple sentences using these words (e.g., "It is a party. I like the fireworks. What do you like to eat? I like the meal.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create simple captions for images of festival foods, demonstrating reading comprehension and vocabulary recall in the "It's Festival Season" scenario.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher provides each student with a sheet containing 3-4 different drawings or images of festival foods (e.g., cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob, a festive meal).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below each image, there are two very short, simple sentences or titles. One is a correct description/caption for the image, and the other is incorrect or unrelated.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Examples:</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Image of cotton candy)
                            <br>A. "I like cotton candy. It is sweet."
                            <br>B. "I like shaved ice. It is cold."
                        </li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Image of corn on the cob)
                            <br>A. "It is a big balloon."
                            <br>B. "This is corn on the cob."
                        </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read both options under each picture and circle the sentence that correctly describes or titles the image.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to read the chosen caption aloud to a partner after circling.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the Production phase and review of completed worksheets for accuracy.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their "Festival Food Captions" worksheet from Stage 4. The teacher will check if students correctly selected the appropriate title/caption for each image.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly matches captions/titles to at least 3 out of 4 images.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows comprehension of simple festival food vocabulary (e.g., cotton candy, shaved ice, corn on the cob).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizes initial letter sounds in target words as observed during choral reading.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions by circling "Yes" or "No" or drawing a happy/sad face:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I read words about festival food?" (Yes / No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I find the right sentence for a picture?" (Yes / No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What festival food word did you like to read the most today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to read? What was difficult?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully identified key vocabulary through reading and matched simple captions. The phonemic awareness activity helped reinforce initial sounds for reading. Some students needed more support with decoding multi-syllable words like "celebration" or "colorful", which suggests continued emphasis on segmenting and blending sounds. The reading comprehension tasks were appropriate for the level. For the next lesson (Speaking), I will ensure opportunities for students to orally describe their favorite festival foods using "I like..." and "It is..." sentences, building on the vocabulary and comprehension from this lesson.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on reading comprehension of festival foods. The next lesson will transition to actively speaking and interacting about these foods and other festival elements, using the vocabulary and simple sentences learned today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>